WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.704 --> 00:00:16.667
young prince wrestles with a dilemma: whether or not to take revenge when his uncle seizes the throne.

2
00:00:17.668 --> 00:00:22.211
This is the story of Hamlet, perhaps William Shakespeare's most famous play.

3
00:00:23.171 --> 00:00:30.735
It begins on a bitterly cold night at Elsinore Castle in Denmark, where two guards, Barnardo and Marcellus,

4
00:00:30.895 --> 00:00:31.616
are keeping watch.

5
00:00:32.672 --> 00:00:33.233
"Who's there?"

6
00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:35.074
called Marcellus warily.

7
00:00:35.554 --> 00:00:37.296
He had spotted someone in the darkness.

8
00:00:37.316 --> 00:00:41.078
"It is I, Horatio," the man replied.

9
00:00:42.139 --> 00:00:48.984
As Horatio approached, Barnardo explained to Marcellus, "I asked him to keep watch with us tonight, in case

10
00:00:49.044 --> 00:00:50.405
that thing reappears.

11
00:00:51.546 --> 00:00:54.128
It seems right, since he is Prince Hamlet's friend."

12
00:00:55.109 --> 00:00:58.992
"Come now, it's surely just your imaginations," said Horatio.

13
00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:08.124
"But we've seen it two nights in a row," insisted Barnardo, "and—" "Shh," interrupted Marcellus, his eyes widening.

14
00:01:08.144 --> 00:01:09.544
"Here it comes again!"

15
00:01:10.805 --> 00:01:13.246
A ghostly figure hovered in the night mist.

16
00:01:14.386 --> 00:01:20.849
Overcoming his fear, Horatio faced the phantom and called out, "Speak, and tell us what kind of thing

17
00:01:20.909 --> 00:01:21.389
you are!"

18
00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:25.151
But the ghost turned away and melted into the night.

19
00:01:25.631 --> 00:01:26.812
Horatio went pale.

20
00:01:27.808 --> 00:01:31.949
"I would not believe this if I hadn't seen it with my own eyes," he murmured.

21
00:01:31.969 --> 00:01:36.610
"It looks like the old king—may he rest in peace," Barnardo said.

22
00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:42.692
"Yes. And it was wearing the same armor our king wore when he slew the king of Norway,"

23
00:01:42.952 --> 00:01:43.812
replied Horatio.

24
00:01:43.832 --> 00:01:48.213
"This ghost may be a sign that violence is coming to Denmark."

25
00:01:48.233 --> 00:01:53.775
"Well, our whole country seems to be preparing for war," said Marcellus.

26
00:01:53.795 --> 00:01:55.015
"Do you know why?"

27
00:01:55.035 --> 00:02:02.018
"Because young Fortinbras, the Norwegian king's son, wants to take back the lands that his father lost to

28
00:02:02.119 --> 00:02:03.579
us," Horatio explained.

29
00:02:03.599 --> 00:02:05.721
"He's gathered an army.

30
00:02:06.221 --> 00:02:10.023
Perhaps that's what's troubling the spirit of the dead king Look!"

31
00:02:11.204 --> 00:02:14.766
The ghost had appeared again, its arms spread wide.

32
00:02:15.866 --> 00:02:17.467
"Stay!" Horatio shouted.

33
00:02:17.487 --> 00:02:20.769
"If you know something about our country's fate, speak!"

34
00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:25.692
Just then a rooster crowed, and the apparition faded away.

35
00:02:26.792 --> 00:02:28.674
"Day is dawning," said Horatio.

36
00:02:28.694 --> 00:02:31.155
"Spirits don't like the light.

37
00:02:31.696 --> 00:02:35.679
Come, we must tell Prince Hamlet that we've seen his father's ghost!"

38
00:02:36.819 --> 00:02:42.404
That morning the new king of Denmark, Claudius, had gathered his court in the castle's great hall.

39
00:02:43.424 --> 00:02:47.908
Claudius was the brother of the old king, who had died only two months earlier.

40
00:02:48.928 --> 00:02:53.672
Claudius had quickly married Gertrude, the king's widow, and so had become king.

41
00:02:54.740 --> 00:03:00.302
"I am sending ambassadors to Norway," Claudius announced, "to discuss the Fortinbras situation."

42
00:03:01.463 --> 00:03:03.003
The king then nodded to Hamlet.

43
00:03:03.444 --> 00:03:09.906
"My nephew and now my son—" "I am not your son," Hamlet muttered under his breath.

44
00:03:09.926 --> 00:03:12.347
"—why do you look so sad?"

45
00:03:12.647 --> 00:03:13.448
continued the king.

46
00:03:14.048 --> 00:03:17.409
"I know you mourn your father, but everyone must die sometime.

47
00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:19.670
Too much grief is not manly.

48
00:03:20.831 --> 00:03:25.221
I realize you want to return to your studies in Germany, but I would prefer you stay in

49
00:03:25.301 --> 00:03:29.843
Elsinore." "Yes," said Queen Gertrude, "please stay, Hamlet."

50
00:03:30.824 --> 00:03:34.966
"I shall obey you, madam," Hamlet replied politely, but he was miserable.

51
00:03:34.986 --> 00:03:38.008
"I cannot understand my mother," he thought.

52
00:03:38.028 --> 00:03:45.212
"I believed she truly loved my father, who was far nobler, fairer, and braver than Claudius.

53
00:03:46.372 --> 00:03:50.855
How could she dishonor my father's memory by so quickly marrying my uncle?"

54
00:03:51.976 --> 00:03:54.037
Just then Horatio entered the hall.

55
00:03:54.057 --> 00:03:57.500
"Horatio," said Hamlet, "I am very glad to see you!"

56
00:03:58.641 --> 00:04:00.262
Horatio drew Hamlet aside.

57
00:04:00.282 --> 00:04:03.905
"My lord," he whispered, "This will shock you.

58
00:04:04.486 --> 00:04:06.768
I saw your father's ghost last night."

59
00:04:07.949 --> 00:04:14.414
"What?" Astonished, Hamlet questioned his friend and then agreed to join the guards and Horatio that evening.

60
00:04:15.355 --> 00:04:17.096
"My father's spirit, in armor!"

61
00:04:17.336 --> 00:04:17.717
he thought.

62
00:04:17.737 --> 00:04:19.258
"All is not well!"

63
00:04:20.339 --> 00:04:26.844
In another part of the castle, Laertes, the son of Polonius, Claudius' chief adviser, was preparing to go

64
00:04:26.884 --> 00:04:27.385
to France.

65
00:04:28.426 --> 00:04:31.789
He went to say good-bye to his sister, Ophelia, in her sewing room.

66
00:04:31.809 --> 00:04:35.232
"Remember to write to me, dear sister," he said.

67
00:04:35.252 --> 00:04:37.493
"Of course," she replied.

68
00:04:37.513 --> 00:04:39.896
"One more thing," he added.

69
00:04:39.916 --> 00:04:44.139
"I know Hamlet's been courting you, but I wouldn't encourage him.

70
00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.621
I don't know if he's serious about you."

71
00:04:47.622 --> 00:04:52.457
Ophelia bristled at her brother's words, but before she could reply, their father entered.

72
00:04:53.457 --> 00:04:57.098
Polonius had a long gray beard and thought himself quite wise.

73
00:04:57.118 --> 00:05:02.179
"Laertes!" he said, "before you leave, I have some advice for you.

74
00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:04.740
Always think before you act.

75
00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:07.321
Do not wear clothes that are too fancy.

76
00:05:07.861 --> 00:05:15.123
Neither borrow nor lend The old man went on for several minutes and concluded with, "And above all,

77
00:05:15.563 --> 00:05:17.183
to thine own self be true."

78
00:05:17.203 --> 00:05:19.284
"I must go, Father!

79
00:05:19.996 --> 00:05:24.719
Ophelia, remember what I said to you," called Laertes as he strode out the door.

80
00:05:24.739 --> 00:05:26.800
"What did he say?"

81
00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:27.941
asked Polonius.

82
00:05:27.961 --> 00:05:31.203
"It was about Prince Hamlet," said Ophelia.

83
00:05:31.223 --> 00:05:32.944
"Ah yes."

84
00:05:33.464 --> 00:05:34.424
The old man nodded.

85
00:05:34.444 --> 00:05:37.226
"I hear he's been spending a lot of time with you.

86
00:05:37.766 --> 00:05:38.567
What is going on?"

87
00:05:39.607 --> 00:05:41.028
"He told me he loves me, Father."

88
00:05:41.048 --> 00:05:43.490
"Hmph," said Polonius.

89
00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:45.171
"Beware of young men!

90
00:05:45.731 --> 00:05:47.192
Do not believe his vows.

91
00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:50.282
And do not waste any more time with him."

92
00:05:51.483 --> 00:05:52.524
Ophelia looked down.

93
00:05:52.544 --> 00:05:56.167
"I shall obey, my lord," she said reluctantly.

94
00:05:57.207 --> 00:06:01.551
That night Hamlet, Horatio, and Marcellus gathered on the guards' platform.

95
00:06:01.571 --> 00:06:03.212
"What time is it?"

96
00:06:03.653 --> 00:06:04.233
asked Hamlet.

97
00:06:04.253 --> 00:06:07.436
"Just past midnight," Marcellus replied.

98
00:06:07.456 --> 00:06:09.718
"The time the spirit usually appears."

99
00:06:10.939 --> 00:06:13.280
"Look!" shouted Horatio, "there it is!"

100
00:06:14.381 --> 00:06:17.384
Hamlet stared as the ghostly figure materialized.

101
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:18.920
"Angels defend us!"

102
00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:19.721
he cried.

103
00:06:19.741 --> 00:06:21.841
"Are you a good spirit or a demon?

104
00:06:22.321 --> 00:06:22.761
Answer me!"

105
00:06:23.822 --> 00:06:26.222
The ghost silently beckoned Hamlet to follow him.

106
00:06:26.242 --> 00:06:29.123
"Don't go," whispered Marcellus fearfully.

107
00:06:29.143 --> 00:06:33.404
"I'm not afraid," declared Hamlet, stepping toward the ghost.

108
00:06:33.424 --> 00:06:35.265
"What if it's evil?"

109
00:06:35.725 --> 00:06:36.425
said Horatio.

110
00:06:36.445 --> 00:06:38.066
"What if it means you harm?"

111
00:06:39.146 --> 00:06:40.786
"Stay!" cried Marcellus.

112
00:06:41.327 --> 00:06:42.707
He grabbed Hamlet's right arm.

113
00:06:43.207 --> 00:06:44.387
Horatio grabbed his left.

114
00:06:45.548 --> 00:06:46.228
"Let go of me!"

115
00:06:46.708 --> 00:06:48.490
roared Hamlet, shaking himself free.

116
00:06:49.531 --> 00:06:51.472
To the ghost, he called out, "Go on.

117
00:06:51.752 --> 00:06:52.433
I'll follow you."

118
00:06:53.614 --> 00:06:57.297
As Hamlet strode after the ghost, Horatio said, "I'm worried.

119
00:06:57.717 --> 00:06:58.498
Let's go after him."

120
00:06:59.119 --> 00:06:59.999
Marcellus nodded.

121
00:07:00.019 --> 00:07:04.363
"Something is rotten in the state of Denmark," he muttered.

122
00:07:05.464 --> 00:07:06.925
The ghost led Hamlet away.

123
00:07:06.945 --> 00:07:14.458
"I am your father's spirit," it said, "doomed to walk the night and spend my days consumed by

124
00:07:14.578 --> 00:07:17.119
fire until my sins are burned away.

125
00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:23.102
Oh Hamlet, if you ever did love me, revenge my terrible murder!"

126
00:07:24.243 --> 00:07:26.444
"Murder?" exclaimed Hamlet, shocked.

127
00:07:27.584 --> 00:07:29.985
"Yes, murder," groaned the ghost.

128
00:07:30.005 --> 00:07:35.108
"People say I was bitten by a snake, but that is false!

129
00:07:36.352 --> 00:07:41.194
The serpent that stung me now wears my crown and has married my wife.

130
00:07:42.435 --> 00:07:47.297
While I was sleeping one day in the garden, your uncle poured a deadly poison in my ear.

131
00:07:48.597 --> 00:07:51.418
It quickly flowed through my blood and killed me.

132
00:07:51.959 --> 00:07:57.721
And so my brother deprived me all at once of life, of crown, of queen.

133
00:07:58.862 --> 00:08:03.283
He ended my life before I could confess my sins and purify my soul!

134
00:08:03.864 --> 00:08:06.369
Hamlet, you must avenge my death!

135
00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:08.691
Farewell, remember me."

136
00:08:09.751 --> 00:08:14.295
The ghost vanished into the mist just as Horatio and Marcellus joined Hamlet.

137
00:08:14.315 --> 00:08:16.176
"What happened, my lord?"

138
00:08:16.596 --> 00:08:17.337
cried Horatio.

139
00:08:18.337 --> 00:08:20.519
Hamlet just stood there, still stunned.

140
00:08:20.539 --> 00:08:23.741
"You must swear to keep it secret," he finally said.

141
00:08:24.902 --> 00:08:27.323
"Swear!" cried the ghost from the darkness.

142
00:08:28.484 --> 00:08:29.285
They all shuddered.

143
00:08:29.805 --> 00:08:34.068
The two men swore secrecy, and Hamlet recounted what the ghost had said.

144
00:08:35.168 --> 00:08:39.770
"From now on," said Hamlet, "I'll pretend to be a little crazy.

145
00:08:40.930 --> 00:08:44.051
Then my uncle won't suspect I'm seeking revenge.

146
00:08:45.132 --> 00:08:49.974
These are terrible times, and I dread what I must do to set them right!"
